  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 769,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 706,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118146,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 6,
        "headingId": 706,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Blood and bone marrow</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T11:34:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 770,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 707,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118147,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 6,
        "headingId": 707,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Patients present with a history of one or more persistent unexplained cytopenias, usually of 4&nbsp;months or longer in duration. When common causes of cytopenias are eliminated, patients are often suspected of having myelodysplastic neoplasm (MDS) or another myeloid disorder. Diagnostic evaluation, including bone marrow examination, will identify a somatic mutation in a CH driver gene or a clonal cytogenetic abnormality, but by definition the case will not meet diagnostic criteria of a defined myeloid neoplasm {26429975; 28424163}. Some cytopenias will be sustained and unexplained without meeting diagnostic criteria for CCUS; such cases should be termed &ldquo;idiopathic cytopenia of unknown significance&rdquo;.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T11:34:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 771,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 708,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118148,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 6,
        "headingId": 708,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>In a large population-based study, anaemia had a prevalence of 4.2%, thrombocytopenia 1.6%, and neutropenia 4.8%. Anaemia and thrombocytopenia increase in prevalence with age, but neutropenia does not {34459888}. Approximately 30% of people with one or more cytopenias will have a detectable mutation in a myeloid driver gene or a chromosomal abnormality, meeting the criteria for CCUS {34255818}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T11:34:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 772,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 709,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118149,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 6,
        "headingId": 709,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The etiology is possibly similar to that of CH. CCUS can occur sporadically or after exposure to cytotoxic treatment {34255818}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T11:34:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 773,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 710,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118150,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 6,
        "headingId": 710,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The pathogenesis is possibly similar to that of CH.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T11:34:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 780,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 717,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118151,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 6,
        "headingId": 717,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T11:34:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 774,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 711,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118152,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 6,
        "headingId": 711,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Bone marrow examination is needed to rule out other causes of cytopenia, including other myeloid neoplasms. Dysplastic changes, if present, must fall short of the diagnostic criteria for MDS. Blasts should not be increased.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T11:34:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 775,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 712,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118153,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 6,
        "headingId": 712,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>See <em>Histopathology</em>, above.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T11:34:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 776,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 713,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118154,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 6,
        "headingId": 713,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>A diagnosis of CCUS requires the identification of a clonal population harbouring one or more CH or myeloid neoplasia&ndash;associated somatic driver mutations or chromosomal alterations. Mutations are typically detected by sequencing DNA from whole blood or bone marrow cells.</p>\r\n<p>Clonal chromosomal abnormalities can be detected by karyotyping, FISH, or next-generation sequencing techniques. The use of array-based techniques or other non-sequencing approaches such as flow cytometry or immunohistochemistry as sole diagnostic modalities to establish a diagnosis of CCUS is not recommended.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T11:34:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 779,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 716,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118155,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 6,
        "headingId": 716,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em> detection of one or more somatic mutations involving CH driver genes listed in <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/63/5/32521\" target=\"_blank\">#32521</a>Table&nbsp;2.02 with a variant allele frequency (VAF) of &ge;&nbsp;2% (&ge;&nbsp;4% for X-linked gene mutations in male patients) in DNA from blood or bone marrow cells, or a clonal chromosomal abnormality; one or more otherwise unexplained persistent cytopenias; absence of features diagnostic for defined myeloid neoplasms on bone marrow examination.</p>\r\n&nbsp;",
        "timestamp": "2024-05-07T15:06:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 777,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 714,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118156,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 6,
        "headingId": 714,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T11:34:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 778,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 715,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118157,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 6,
        "headingId": 715,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The risk of progression from CCUS to a frank myeloid neoplasm, particularly MDS, increases with the size of the clonal population, the number of somatic alterations present {33436578}, and the presence of specific gene mutations such as those in <em>TP53</em>, <em>PPM1D</em>, <em>JAK2</em>, <em>RUNX1</em>, <em>SF3B1</em>, <em>SRSF2</em>, <em>U2AF1</em>, <em>IDH2</em>, and <em>IDH1</em> {28424163}. The number and severity of cytopenias may also be a risk factor for clinical progression, particularly in patients with a prior history of exposure to cytotoxic therapy {33106634; 34423258}. Isolated mutation in <em>DNMT3A</em> seems to confer a low risk of progression to MDS or acute myeloid leukaemia {34255818}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T11:34:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]